xplanation":"The as ... as pattern uses the base adjective bad, not a comparative or superlative.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"equality","contextKey":"adjective-bad","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-comparisons","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"62f39894be1e93a01f7a9693b839e3010a562b1f17b9fdc5fd4996dfa69f57b0","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **equality**. Many short adjectives add -er; longer adjectives often use more. Introduce the second item with than. Avoid adding more to an -er comparative.","The as ... as pattern uses the base adjective bad, not a comparative or superlative.","Choose **bad**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An equal comparison uses **as + adjective + as**. **worst** does not supply the missing part of that pattern. The as ... as pattern uses the base adjective bad, not a comparative or superlative.","B":"An equal comparison uses **as + adjective + as**. **more worse** does not supply the missing part of that pattern. The as ... as pattern uses the base adjective bad, not a comparative or superlative.","C":"The as ... as pattern uses the base adjective bad, not a comparative or superlative.","D":"An equal comparison uses **as + adjective + as**. **worse** does not supply the missing part of that pattern. The as ... as pattern uses the base adjective bad, not a comparative or superlative."}}},{"id":"bbbd914b-c461-5fad-ab1b-9228b90a7f80","topicSlug":"comparatives-and-superlatives","level":"A2","prompt":"The first of these two mistakes is worse ____ the second.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"then"},{"id":"B","text":"as"},{"id":"C","text":"that"},{"id":"D","text":"than"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Many short adjectives add -er; longer adjectives often use more. Introduce the second item with than. Avoid adding more to an -er comparative. Focus on **than** in this question.","explanation":"After the comparative worse, use than to introduce the item being compared.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"than","contextKey":"adjective-bad","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-comparisons","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"76343a6744eb458d9139df2a0938c461cb956604c825d8620eada5041ad2f23c","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **than**. Many short adjectives add -er; longer adjectives often use more. Introduce the second item with than. Avoid adding more to an -er comparative.","After the comparative worse, use than to introduce the item being compared.","Choose **than**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The target of an ordinary comparative is introduced by **than**, not **then**. As belongs to an equality pattern; that is a different connector.","B":"The target of an ordinary comparative is introduced by **than**, not **as**. As belongs to an equality pattern; that is a different connector.","C":"The target of an ordinary comparative is introduced by **than**, not **that**. As belongs to an equality pattern; that is a different connector.","D":"After the comparative worse, use than to introduce the item being compared."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["A comparative compares one thing with another; a superlative places one member at an extreme within a group. Equal comparisons use as ... as. First choose the kind of comparison, then form the adjective correctly. The adjective does not agree with a noun’s number."],"rules":[{"title":"Comparing two","explanation":"Many short adjectives add -er; longer adjectives often use more. Introduce the second item with than. Avoid adding more to an -er comparative.","examples":["This route is shorter than that one.","The new seat is more comfortable.","This route is **shorter than** the coastal road."],"pattern":"small → small**er** → small**est**","paragraphs":["Many short adjectives take -er and -est. Spelling may change: big → bigger, happy → happier. Many longer adjectives use more and most. Some two-syllable adjectives allow both patterns, so avoid treating every alternative as wrong without checking usage."]},{"title":"Comparing a group","explanation":"Use the plus a superlative: often -est or most plus the adjective. Good becomes better/best; bad becomes worse/worst.","examples":["This is the safest path.","That was the best result.","The two routes are **as safe as** each other."],"pattern":"interesting → **more** interesting → **most** interesting","paragraphs":["Good → better → best and bad → worse → worst are irregular. Farther and further have overlapping uses, with further also meaning additional. Do not put more before an adjective that already has the ordinary comparative ending: more taller is not standard."]},{"title":"Equal degree","explanation":"Use as plus the base adjective plus as for equality. Spelling may change in comparative and superlative forms.","examples":["The two boxes are as heavy as each other.","big → bigger → biggest","This is **the most direct** route of the three."],"pattern":"**as tall as** · **taller than** · **the tallest in** the group","paragraphs":["Use than to introduce the comparison target after a comparative. Use as on both sides of the adjective for equality. Superlatives usually take the, but possessives can replace it: my best friend. A superlative needs an understood comparison group."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Much, far and a little modify comparatives: much faster, a little slower. Very faster is not the ordinary pattern. A comparative can be used without an explicit than phrase if the reference is understood: This one is better. Not as ... as expresses inequality without a comparative ending.","examples":["This route is **shorter than** the coastal road.","The two routes are **as safe as** each other.","This is **the most direct** route of the three."]}],"recap":["small → small**er** → small**est**","interesting → **more** interesting → **most** interesting","**as tall as** · **taller than** · **the tallest in** the group"]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["ed-ing-adjectives"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
